Here's one way to create a CSS code that styles a button with a bright green background and white text:

```css
.green-button {
  background-color: #00ff00;
  color: white;
  padding: 10px 20px;
  border: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  font-size: 16px;
}
.green-button:hover {
  background-color: #008f00;
}
```

This code creates a new class called `green-button` that can be applied to any button element. The `background-color` property sets the background color of the button to a bright green, while the `color` property sets the text color to white. 

The `padding` property adds some space around the text within the button, making it more visually appealing. The `border` property is set to `none` to remove any default borders that the button might have. 

The `cursor` property is set to `pointer` to change the cursor to a hand when the user hovers over the button, indicating that the button is clickable. The `font-size` property is used to increase the size of the text within the button.

Finally, a `hover` pseudo-class is added to change the background color of the button to a darker shade of green when the user hovers over it, providing visual feedback that the button is interactive.

